vlib/runtime/free_memory_impl_darwin.c.v already declares mach_host_self,
host_page_size and host_statistics64, and V's `fn C.` declarations are
program-wide, so vfetch's own declarations lose to vlib's and its call sites
no longer typecheck. Drop the duplicates and follow the same calling
convention vlib itself uses: a u32 page size cast to &C.vm_size_t, the info
struct cast to &int, and a &u32 count.

Using the C macros for the flavour and count also removes the hardcoded
values, which were only correct for one SDK.

This variant covers macOS 10.5 through 10.8 (Darwin < 13).

10.5 has neither host_statistics64 nor vm_statistics64 -- both arrived in
10.6, so 10.5 has no other option. But 10.6, 10.7 and 10.8 also use this
32-bit path, even though their SDK's vm_statistics64 struct exists: vfetch's
own copy of that struct (in the upstream source this patch replaces) is the
24-field, 10.9+ shape with the memory-compression counters, and building it
on an older SDK whose <mach/vm_statistics.h> only defines the 15-field
pre-compression struct fails outright --
  cc: ...vfetch.*.tmp.c: error: variable '_t2' has initializer but incomplete type
  cc: ...vfetch.*.tmp.c: error: 'struct vm_statistics64' has no member named 'free_count'
-- rather than hand-trim that struct to match each SDK (its fields are a
mix of 32- and 64-bit types, easy to get subtly wrong), every pre-10.9
target uses the classic 32-bit host_statistics/HOST_VM_INFO interface
instead. That struct is unchanged from Mac OS X 10.0 through today (its
own header comment: "you cannot add any new fields to this structure. Add
them below in vm_statistics64"), gained purgeable_count and
speculative_count in 10.5, and vlib does not declare host_statistics, so it
is declared here in vlib's style. 32-bit page counts cannot overflow on
hardware this old (a 32-bit count tops out around 16TB of RAM at a 4KB page
size).

kern.memorystatus_vm_pressure_level does not exist before 10.9 either;
return none.

